    "turn_text": "they say? My mother and my father were so afraid, especially my mother. She thought that I would get hurt, that the house would be bombed or burned, and that we could lose the land. And my mother didn't want to have anything to do with my interest in a tenant's choice, a state. So I went after school in Nashville, Tennessee. Anonka of mine gave me a $100 bill. More money than I ever had. It gave me a footlocker. I put everything that I own in that footlock. My clothes in, my few books, accepted chickens that I used to raise as a child, and took a Greyhound bus to Nashville, Tennessee, where I studied for four years. But while studying there, I started attending non-violent workshops. And one of the King Jr. would come there to speak. And I would see him and I would talk with him. And I got involved in the movement and he knew I was getting involved. And he encouraged me and suggested when I finished college and got my education that I should return to Alabama to work with him and the people in the movement.",
