    "turn_text": "because you just knew. Well, I thought we would be arrested and we would be going to jail. So I wanted to have something to read. I wanted to have something to eat since I was going to be in jail with my friends and colleagues and neighbors. I wanted to be repressed my teeth. All these many years later, I don't know what happened to the backpack, to the apple, the orange, or the toothpaste, or the toothbrush. But we had to march that day. We had to walk across that bridge. It's the Eton Pettis Bridge, crossing the Alabama River. We were on our way from Selma to Montgomery to dramatize to the nation and to the world. that the black people in the black belt of Alabama wanted to register the vote to participate in the democratic process. People had to pass a so-called deficit test. People were told they could not read or write well now. People were asked to count the number of bubbles in a bar soap, the number of jelly beans in a jar. There were African-American lawyers and doctors, college professors, teachers who flunked the so-called literacy tests. We had to do it.",
