    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/3/73253.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 44,
    "turn_text": "We got on the other side of the bridge. It was a group of state troopers standing in the major said the Alabama State Police. This is an unlawful march. It would not be allowed to continue. I give you three minutes to disperse and return to your homes or to your church. And one of the guys walking with me leading the march by the name of Jose William said major give us a moment to kneel and pray. The major said again, triple advance. I said, major, man, have a word. You said there would be no word. Troopers advance. You saw these guys putting on a gas mask. They came toward us, beating us with night sticks, shramping us with horses, and sitting here by state triple with the night stick. My legs went from under me. I thought I saw death. I thought I was going to die. And to this day, I don't know how I made it back across that bridge through the streets of Selma back to the little church where we had left for. But I do remember being back at the church. They asked me to assess something. And I stood up and said, I don't understand it. President Johnson can send troops to Vietnam. And can I send troops to Selma, Alabama, to protect people whom it desires to register to vote? I was asked for a lie with 16 other people. And a group of nuns took care of us.",
